#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078341 :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905 :topic/difficulty 1.517971234312146 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428147 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 2 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1, 2]; waits [1, 1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (2/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1179->1061. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380} :encompassing/weight 1.0} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428148 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1179->466. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "701ed6eb-4bd0-5344-bf15-d9a52b59f3af" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100894}] :topic/math-academy-id 1179 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050895}] :topic/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Separation of Variables"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725849 :question-generator/id #uuid "fd50bfb5-0d60-5ce1-8dee-34308cdd4ee1" :question-generator/script "generators/14/14a636101b2ee8e8eaa5611ac07421334a56085ad9c55001208166fc0cdf5d94/17592186078584.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7840ac-4dc2-5743-a317-909d0854aa2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273200} {:answer/id #uuid "80a9183c-594a-50ff-89ee-e5298cd3be92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273490} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc2c9b-f809-527a-9228-06e37aeb1131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273553} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fc440f-ad27-570f-b514-432bec694cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15396bb-b0f6-5ba4-9f17-f68ca77b61da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275003}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b0ed3c5-b3aa-5668-ae2c-48b159839332" :question/math-academy-id "q-15384" :question/problem "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1495237b-afbf-5205-a022-9f6acc5cbda9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272570} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8e38af-c5d0-507f-a1bd-aae08b27500d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273439} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f377d8-6bb6-5509-84d5-efce6120f034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273947} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd7cc63-77c2-5834-8ea5-22b666b6543c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274316} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4ed67-f97a-54dc-81d8-37f939ad8898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbd7cc63-77c2-5834-8ea5-22b666b6543c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274316} :answer-field/id #uuid "217e660e-5140-5d1b-956d-f278238bb223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274697}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "daa15db5-c1a1-535c-9031-79b2bb1e03e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15595" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e0dff5-bfb1-5212-94c6-b1dadc022a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241760} {:answer/id #uuid "45e54fee-1ace-542a-8f41-af48ff15ba39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241808} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9fc5e0-ba4e-56c2-8bd8-2c6ee24e4cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242007} {:answer/id #uuid "a245084d-1bb7-5412-bc76-5d4162c98715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{76}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242601} {:answer/id #uuid "d36cea6e-e345-5856-9738-8be126828c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40e0dff5-bfb1-5212-94c6-b1dadc022a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241760} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa52215b-9a2a-5e79-b263-c1fe67ce6a74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243745}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b60de53-dea5-5dd2-908e-421b529445e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-107118" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${140}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $5$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-0.1\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-\\int 0.1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-0.1t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-0.1t+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-0.1t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${140}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =28+K \\\\ K & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+112{e}^{-0.1t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=5$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(5)=28+112{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 5}≈{96}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04eda6ac-1a0c-52d3-accd-9e4287c8ed9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241219} {:answer/id #uuid "1769c61b-0297-5fb6-9734-e3f5580daf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241380} {:answer/id #uuid "65c47c3d-f35e-5489-ade5-8d1e2e7968d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242096} {:answer/id #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfe89bb-415b-5c7b-84d6-7bacf9027ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} :answer-field/id #uuid "393a690d-1e4c-5074-9abc-2b65ddbc8354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243547}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f919d480-6159-52e5-bf01-76d804f5fa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-107120" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123a42f8-2a35-5617-ba7d-f1b4d2c746b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241335} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc08283-0067-51f0-81a0-558fa4cdcf8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242008} {:answer/id #uuid "8346bdeb-1005-53c8-8841-f50949f617dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242327} {:answer/id #uuid "9c820d27-170e-5c1f-81bb-f4a2542fecd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242546} {:answer/id #uuid "ad515dcc-0231-5912-ac0e-df7d88012a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8346bdeb-1005-53c8-8841-f50949f617dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242327} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbd68ea-f66a-5917-98d1-898732b44fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39da46ca-e83e-580d-8039-34194e24ef5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107122" :question/problem "A thermometer is taken out of a room where the air temperature is ${72}^{∘}F$  and brought outside where the temperature is ${15}^{∘}F,$ and its reading begins to decrease. After $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}F.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${25}^{∘}F?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{57}\\right) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{57}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{10}{57}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{10}{57}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{10}{57}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{57}\\right)}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ae70af-887b-5770-9ba5-23db38cd4801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241332} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbae164-802b-58ab-91b2-3faf9544773d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241579} {:answer/id #uuid "4231eca7-f91a-5287-acdd-0be0665d65cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{43}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241773} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d27d97-2c83-55f0-970f-d85dcbf228a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242597} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d7b5b-1863-5704-89a4-3e2c90453ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cbae164-802b-58ab-91b2-3faf9544773d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241579} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec7d5808-b789-5047-9d21-ccfd0fbce57b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243845}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ff863d3-8088-512b-858b-b0b33302607f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107129" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.12\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.12t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.12t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =21+K \\\\ K & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+44{e}^{-0.12t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=21+44{e}^{-0.12\\cdot 8}≈{38}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "893f4780-2c8b-522b-8087-2fc24822ebd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395764} {:answer/id #uuid "93cd5cdd-b047-544f-b7e7-fee685cf62f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395765} {:answer/id #uuid "ef24ffa0-3d6e-5335-9009-9906ff059c99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395766} {:answer/id #uuid "d38f97ca-3e74-5b74-90a6-ef1513732aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395767} {:answer/id #uuid "96517c6b-bae9-503f-9f98-d3f004b861a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93cd5cdd-b047-544f-b7e7-fee685cf62f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395765} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca47f9c-32af-5d7e-92a5-e9a1ab61bc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395763}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02ce9872-8986-5e67-bea1-b49d589c24ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-15589" :question/problem "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${25}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=90,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =25+75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 65 & =75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{13}{15} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{13}{15}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{13}{15}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 25+75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{13}{15}\\right)}≈95\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12647afe-fb4f-5f2b-9326-19bf07c40a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397886} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd2a414-b8e6-52ba-9a39-7d6bbb93cce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397887} {:answer/id #uuid "6612145e-ab64-58c5-8ac9-574cb5ec22a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397888} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed38fbd-9ab4-5313-a9e7-428d7d2e672f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e45ac-2ae6-5e93-b31c-67cedb5a0fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ed38fbd-9ab4-5313-a9e7-428d7d2e672f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad59f961-3401-5e50-8b21-92d3facaf654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397885}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee906ee-def7-5194-a87e-a1f77108aa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107125" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339a07cb-65ed-5553-99c1-8ac87a3d2893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{98}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486705} {:answer/id #uuid "43b562cd-2db1-5728-88b6-e6241152ba5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{126}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486741} {:answer/id #uuid "9d515764-afcd-5111-817f-5442e35ae0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{112}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486947} {:answer/id #uuid "b82eed89-d5fa-5f72-8196-eb6c23acabba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486997} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9f3273-c38d-5046-8ba2-d45f97de8bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{134}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b562cd-2db1-5728-88b6-e6241152ba5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{126}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486741} :answer-field/id #uuid "3746981f-0e79-5e47-8df3-c9e044188fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487160}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "533feaf7-7a54-5e3c-8829-f3324ed43a28" :question/math-academy-id "q-107128" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of an exhaust pipe of a motorcycle can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe is ${140}^{∘}F,$ calculate its temperature after $3$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the exhaust pipe of a motorcycle is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-0.08\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-\\int 0.08\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-74| & =-0.08t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-74>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-74 & ={e}^{-0.08t+C} \\\\ T-74 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T-74 & =K{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T & =74+K{e}^{-0.08t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe was ${140}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =74+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =74+K \\\\ K & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=74+66{e}^{-0.08t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the exhaust pipe after $t=3$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(3)=74+66{e}^{-0.08\\cdot 3}≈{126}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136b59f4-a9e4-5d8c-b869-dad46630033a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529422} {:answer/id #uuid "461e25d2-998f-5231-9ad9-048327e6eb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529433} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce962ca-5f25-5bd0-8fc8-ea3116721951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529450} {:answer/id #uuid "90c189d5-a421-5b5e-906b-c9e5138b97ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529452} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab3b094-2f4a-5213-b0de-26f0f8cd39f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "136b59f4-a9e4-5d8c-b869-dad46630033a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529422} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c3cd81-387f-52f6-8598-4e98964b059c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529453}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08518a50-98e2-5d9c-a6f5-59dc1a4a2277" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${18}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${60}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $3$ minutes and ${39}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-18),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-18} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-18} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-18| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-18>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-18 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-18 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-18 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =18+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(3)=60$ and $T(7)=39$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =18+Ke^{-3k} \\\\ 42 & =Ke^{-3k} \\\\ 39 & =18+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 21 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{42} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-3k}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =e^{-4k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =-4k \\\\ k & =\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(3)=60$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}42 & =Ke^{-3\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)} \\\\ K & =42e^{3\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)} \\\\ K & =42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=18+42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =18+(42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}})e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 3}=60 \\\\ T(7) & =18+(42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}})e^{-\\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 7}=39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 18+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =7 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{42\\left(\\frac{42}{21}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{4}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =3+4\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}≈13\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2061e414-b4ea-5c57-ad60-fc9a5c241449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529425} {:answer/id #uuid "3148407a-a1b7-5be3-ae1b-0b7c3252c208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529429} {:answer/id #uuid "642227aa-f6b5-5f2c-9789-1258fcc2941b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529439} {:answer/id #uuid "81dae93e-910d-5d6a-839a-efb7b838187c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529447} {:answer/id #uuid "84a07af3-e9fe-57c9-9b86-f82e9bbbe6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3148407a-a1b7-5be3-ae1b-0b7c3252c208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529429} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2a34ec-9222-55be-9af3-842e7b4fe46d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529457}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55811d49-71e0-57b3-9d4a-d1c8d9d4f58f" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${12}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $1$ minute and ${49}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $4$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${19}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-12),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-12| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-12>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-12 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-12 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-12 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =12+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(1)=64$ and $T(4)=49$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =12+Ke^{-1k} \\\\ 52 & =Ke^{-1k} \\\\ 49 & =12+Ke^{-4k} \\\\ 37 & =Ke^{-4k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{52} & =\\frac{Ke^{-4k}}{Ke^{-1k}} \\\\ \\frac{37}{52} & =e^{-3k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{37}{52}\\right) & =-3k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(1)=64$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =Ke^{-1\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ K & =52e^{1\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)} \\\\ K & =52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=12+52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =12+(52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1}=64 \\\\ T(4) & =12+(52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 4}=49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${19}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =19 \\\\ 12+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =19 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =7 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{37}{52} \\right)}}{3}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{52\\left(\\frac{52}{37}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =1+3\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{52}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{37}{52}\\right)}≈19\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035a9a44-7a16-5905-a0dc-77a5358cf6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529417} {:answer/id #uuid "09046438-fbef-58e9-b053-5d1dfaf0a2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529419} {:answer/id #uuid "230162fd-bc9d-51be-85ce-5f37c00f2f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529427} {:answer/id #uuid "73a7a8ba-22d0-5b55-b3ed-cf04f2684bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529443} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb1a3a5-87a1-5709-994d-fc4337d0f0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09046438-fbef-58e9-b053-5d1dfaf0a2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529419} :answer-field/id #uuid "21da3e90-7194-505b-be0e-0a0a44ad7031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529426}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57bd2c5d-2ec7-5e40-ac6f-231dd6ea513e" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${23}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${110}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${62}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $8$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-23),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-23>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=110$ and $T(8)=62$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}110 & =23+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 87 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 62 & =23+Ke^{-8k} \\\\ 39 & =Ke^{-8k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{39}{87} & =\\frac{Ke^{-8k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{29} & =e^{-6k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{29}\\right) & =-6k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=110$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}87 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)} \\\\ K & =87e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)} \\\\ K & =87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=23+87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =23+(87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)\\cdot 2}=110 \\\\ T(8) & =23+(87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}\\right)\\cdot 8}=62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 23+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{29} \\right)}}{6}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{87\\left(\\frac{87}{39}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+6\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{2}{87}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{29}\\right)}≈30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108ed220-ff0d-5a3e-9732-871db25e264d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529421} {:answer/id #uuid "36b98352-a45c-5756-ba26-e8f13b324484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529430} {:answer/id #uuid "40afc4ac-32fd-5122-8320-a1b94bc430b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529431} {:answer/id #uuid "64fd9fd9-a113-5826-9e9f-9bf1153c8645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529440} {:answer/id #uuid "751594c1-eba1-5f1c-a604-af02b2e24eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b98352-a45c-5756-ba26-e8f13b324484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529430} :answer-field/id #uuid "f54cefd1-735e-592f-a832-09bdfef2567e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529463}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6178d66f-8125-5a9f-a46c-197579fd4dfe" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${14}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${114}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${79}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${32}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-14),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-14} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-14} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-14| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-14>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-14 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-14 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-14 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =14+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=114$ and $T(7)=79$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}114 & =14+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 100 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 79 & =14+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 65 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{65}{100} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{20} & =e^{-5k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{20}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=114$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =100e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=14+100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =14+(100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 2}=114 \\\\ T(7) & =14+(100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 7}=79\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${32}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =32 \\\\ 14+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =32 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =18 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{18}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{20} \\right)}}{5}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{18}{100\\left(\\frac{100}{65}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+5\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{13}{20}\\right)}≈22\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b59f9f-0328-5e3a-b3a0-9ae94cd9029b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529437} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc12c7b-7d04-5703-959a-c62329b886f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529442} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed0441f-d7c8-59ac-b964-78a874544707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529451} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c8bae0-5d37-5021-8cbd-592d9fbcffea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529459} {:answer/id #uuid "c9905343-f98e-5172-a713-e8ad676df9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ed0441f-d7c8-59ac-b964-78a874544707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529451} :answer-field/id #uuid "56bac0d5-d078-5477-b2fe-4ca3e2469c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bca55c5-9e2a-5abf-bde2-2e93390e2953" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${59}^{∘}C.$ After $2$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${51}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${26}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${12}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={12}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-12).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-12} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-12| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-12>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-12 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-12 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-12 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =12+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${59}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=59,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}59 & =12+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 59 & =12+K \\\\ K & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=12+47{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${51}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=51,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =12+47{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =47{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{47} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${26}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =26 \\\\ 12+47{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =26 \\\\ 47{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =14 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{14}{47} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${26}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{14}{47}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{39}{47}\\right)}≈13\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14340294-f6e6-5843-a6fe-4ec754aa2b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529423} {:answer/id #uuid "26c1b56e-8363-5b7a-9cd7-fc35a89b4352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529428} {:answer/id #uuid "42a5f854-c421-5906-9531-dfb338eb3d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529432} {:answer/id #uuid "68bd5f21-2fc4-57db-ab21-cd5920a10ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529441} {:answer/id #uuid "b750d51c-f2a0-5a6c-a1e6-ddae4ed1f957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14340294-f6e6-5843-a6fe-4ec754aa2b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529423} :answer-field/id #uuid "773705ba-e6bc-585e-ac23-61ef9313aea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83a0fd6e-0040-59af-810e-aa17184cf1d7" :question/problem "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${17}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $3$ minutes, it cools to ${83}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${43}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={17}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-17).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-17} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-17} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-17| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-17>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-17 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-17 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-17 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =17+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =17+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =17+K \\\\ K & =83\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=17+83{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $3$ minutes, it cools to ${83}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(3)=83,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}83 & =17+83{e}^{-3k} \\\\ 66 & =83{e}^{-3k} \\\\ \\frac{66}{83} & ={e}^{-3k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right) & =-3k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${43}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =43 \\\\ 17+83{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =43 \\\\ 83{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =26 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{26}{83} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${43}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{26}{83}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{66}{83}\\right)}≈15\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db77da5-938c-5d21-b72a-0f710e5c2a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529420} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb8c333-7a33-50bf-877e-90ce08332112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529424} {:answer/id #uuid "9a43cb5e-136c-5d82-8309-2ec9f8922e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529454} {:answer/id #uuid "9efca7bd-9559-5f53-b339-6146e7712ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529456} {:answer/id #uuid "f372127d-3efe-5b72-a30e-572b7ee9abc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f372127d-3efe-5b72-a30e-572b7ee9abc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529462} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ae2599-3eaa-5407-bab7-c011910aa3c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa306e16-1691-5350-96e5-61b0e9060130" :question/problem "A heated metal body cools in a room at ${22}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Its temperature is ${82}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $2$ minutes and ${54}^{∘}\\text{C}$ after $7$ minutes. The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, obeys Newton's law of cooling, with $t$ measured in minutes since cooling began. To the nearest minute, how long after cooling began does the body reach ${31}^{∘}\\text{C}$?" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-22),\\qquad k>0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-22} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-22} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-22| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-22>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-22 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-22 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-22 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =22+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nApply the two observed conditions $T(2)=82$ and $T(7)=54$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}82 & =22+Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 60 & =Ke^{-2k} \\\\ 54 & =22+Ke^{-7k} \\\\ 32 & =Ke^{-7k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the excess temperatures to eliminate $K$, then take natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{60} & =\\frac{Ke^{-7k}}{Ke^{-2k}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15} & =e^{-5k} \\\\ \\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{15}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute this rate into $T(2)=82$ to determine the constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =Ke^{-2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =60e^{2\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)} \\\\ K & =60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution satisfying both observations is\n\n$$\nT(t)=22+60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two observation times confirms the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =22+(60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 2}=82 \\\\ T(7) & =22+(60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}})e^{-\\left(- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}\\right)\\cdot 7}=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the body reaches ${31}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =31 \\\\ 22+Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =31 \\\\ Ke^{-k{t}_{1}} & =9 \\\\ e^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{9}{K} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{K}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{K}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the values of $K$ and $k$ to obtain the time measured since cooling began:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(\\frac{8}{15} \\right)}}{5}}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{60\\left(\\frac{60}{32}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{5}}}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2+5\\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{3}{20}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{8}{15}\\right)}≈17\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0567bc3f-9df8-555c-9eb4-4877b8ad08f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646436} {:answer/id #uuid "47771db8-78e6-59e2-b53e-aae2adeb3b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646541} {:answer/id #uuid "658304df-2c24-562b-ba9c-46810e0ee683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646609} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfdadce-247d-59d5-bebf-ac00fdbc2cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646623} {:answer/id #uuid "fe828fde-8fe3-568c-b775-f98fa439bbf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "658304df-2c24-562b-ba9c-46810e0ee683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646609} :answer-field/id #uuid "d35bfdda-4adb-54fe-b087-fcfe2627f7e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4360bcb1-959b-53b1-855f-91f5d582be11" :question/math-academy-id "q-107123" :question/problem "A pork leg is taken out of the oven with a temperature of ${150}^{∘}C,$ and placed in a room that maintains a temperature of ${24}^{∘}C$, and its temperature begins to decrease. After $15$ minutes, the  temperature of the pork leg is ${100}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the pork leg to have a temperature of ${60}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the pork leg decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the pork leg was transferred into the ${24}^{∘}C$ room.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={24}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-24).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-24} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-24} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-24| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-24>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-24 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-24 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-24 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =24+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the pork leg was ${150}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=150,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150 & =24+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 150 & =24+K \\\\ K & =126\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=24+126{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $15$ minutes, the temperature of the pork leg is ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(15)=100,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =24+126{e}^{-15k} \\\\ 76 & =126{e}^{-15k} \\\\ \\frac{38}{63} & ={e}^{-15k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right) & =-15k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the pork leg has a temperature of ${60}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =60 \\\\ 24+126{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =60 \\\\ 126{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =36 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the pork leg will have a temperature of ${60}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right)}≈37\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f9765f-cae1-5047-89d8-11e4a7dfe9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646464} {:answer/id #uuid "3403c6e3-ec8a-53c5-a425-cc33f931d370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{34}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646512} {:answer/id #uuid "34661e1b-3a93-50b1-ae0f-ea69ac80e885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646514} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3f2cd0-e2b5-5544-8182-2ed4047d9fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646528} {:answer/id #uuid "be5ba072-dbbf-558e-8149-4e7211637e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{36}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be5ba072-dbbf-558e-8149-4e7211637e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{36}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646767} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33ab9c8-fcf0-5f44-afca-e7fe6606a08c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646996}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44b64320-501f-5d6a-b8c7-b8cf6a960fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-107121" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a body can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.06(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the body is ${40}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the body after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the body is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.06(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.06\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.06\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.06t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.06t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.06t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.06t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.06t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the body was ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=40,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 40 & =21+K \\\\ K & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+19{e}^{-0.06t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the body after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4)=21+19{e}^{-0.06\\cdot 4}≈{36}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be068eb-64c8-59a3-afa7-5664f17d1b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{115}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672735} {:answer/id #uuid "14ca0aad-76fb-51da-8fca-64986833086a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{109}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672738} {:answer/id #uuid "6163c0dc-e2e5-5bc5-822c-c053738923b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{116}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672747} {:answer/id #uuid "708c015e-8ffa-5dd3-8bdf-3b6335424caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{111}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672751} {:answer/id #uuid "96a3750a-e8ce-5fd8-a114-550474ae2c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{113}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14ca0aad-76fb-51da-8fca-64986833086a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{109}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672738} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd6a476-2f32-513b-b9d7-56d18d395036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672736}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bcfe524-d1bc-5602-9bbb-6250eda84ba0" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.03(T-30)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${119}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.03(T-30).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-30} & =-0.03\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-30} & =-\\int 0.03\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-30| & =-0.03t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-30>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-30 & ={e}^{-0.03t+C} \\\\ T-30 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\ T-30 & =K{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\ T & =30+K{e}^{-0.03t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${119}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=119,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}119 & =30+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 119 & =30+K \\\\ K & =89\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=30+89{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4)=30+89{e}^{-0.03\\cdot 4}≈{109}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c113558-955c-5204-a915-d21433372a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672740} {:answer/id #uuid "34e055a3-ef9f-5de7-b337-ea388b3368b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{39}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672741} {:answer/id #uuid "59d7abd4-e8b4-5fb6-9c0e-a26d63d65981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672745} {:answer/id #uuid "69df54c2-ee15-5a9b-a0b9-ba288365db68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{43}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672748} {:answer/id #uuid "81e12548-0078-542b-a7be-ebccf5ee0831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c113558-955c-5204-a915-d21433372a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672740} :answer-field/id #uuid "d517d6ae-4a01-559e-a78d-ff71eb2425d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672762}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20cf5dff-13d7-53c1-80a8-f2d47d353696" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a body can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-33)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the body is ${55}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the body after $9$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the body is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-33).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-33} & =-0.08\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-33} & =-\\int 0.08\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-33| & =-0.08t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-33>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-33 & ={e}^{-0.08t+C} \\\\ T-33 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T-33 & =K{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T & =33+K{e}^{-0.08t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the body was ${55}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=55,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}55 & =33+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 55 & =33+K \\\\ K & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=33+22{e}^{-0.08t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the body after $t=9$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(9)=33+22{e}^{-0.08\\cdot 9}≈{44}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0496ce21-94ad-5059-9245-6e93db58d821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672732} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3555e7-76cc-58b7-b084-57a457f82ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672742} {:answer/id #uuid "d337d87b-784c-5884-85d9-dde8eef8b832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{61}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672761} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e45505-5515-5a20-97a9-f1eaa6ec8d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{53}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672764} {:answer/id #uuid "e54193fa-674b-57bd-b84b-d76431906b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{59}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f3555e7-76cc-58b7-b084-57a457f82ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672742} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b78b1b8-fbe7-50fe-a450-31525c7b8ea4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672759}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a5a1d59-ffa3-514b-bca9-b5126ed8e4bf" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-34)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${96}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $12$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-34).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-34} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-34} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-34| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-34>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-34 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-34 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-34 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =34+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${96}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=96,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}96 & =34+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 96 & =34+K \\\\ K & =62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=34+62{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=12$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(12)=34+62{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 12}≈{55}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08567872-9496-590d-8e6f-b30e71703fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672733} {:answer/id #uuid "43c21da3-ea72-5125-be19-3be6a575a8f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672743} {:answer/id #uuid "6acbf7bf-3831-5363-bd01-e7054b0eadf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672749} {:answer/id #uuid "78871fa2-3d48-5d31-a760-6b331565db36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672753} {:answer/id #uuid "9363bab4-0b88-5da7-9187-b6087fb174fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78871fa2-3d48-5d31-a760-6b331565db36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{84}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672753} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f37b8e-125b-5fb2-bef1-4f975e2143e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672760}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7220076f-7a8f-59d7-a472-6fa27abeba93" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.15(T-34)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${176}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $7$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.15(T-34).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-34} & =-0.15\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-34} & =-\\int 0.15\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-34| & =-0.15t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-34>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-34 & ={e}^{-0.15t+C} \\\\ T-34 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.15t} \\\\ T-34 & =K{e}^{-0.15t} \\\\ T & =34+K{e}^{-0.15t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${176}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=176,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}176 & =34+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 176 & =34+K \\\\ K & =142\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=34+142{e}^{-0.15t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=7$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(7)=34+142{e}^{-0.15\\cdot 7}≈{84}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13855e76-e295-5d9a-8d11-1c293c24822e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{57}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672737} {:answer/id #uuid "535f5b1f-e65c-5148-9576-c6d54a0f7ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{56}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672744} {:answer/id #uuid "6f49ad19-3571-500e-bae9-8b2b72dc7975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{53}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672750} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2ff935-e641-5936-a4ec-1a5e21cee286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672754} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ddc331-dc9a-5453-bcbd-7038f002203f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{52}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f49ad19-3571-500e-bae9-8b2b72dc7975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{53}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672750} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b2f37f-52a6-5af2-a3b8-26d28c39951d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672763}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "980a5957-ed65-5e73-85e5-566f07d47b61" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.07(T-34)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $7$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.07(T-34).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-34} & =-0.07\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-34} & =-\\int 0.07\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-34| & =-0.07t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-34>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-34 & ={e}^{-0.07t+C} \\\\ T-34 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.07t} \\\\ T-34 & =K{e}^{-0.07t} \\\\ T & =34+K{e}^{-0.07t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the bulb was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =34+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =34+K \\\\ K & =31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=34+31{e}^{-0.07t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=7$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(7)=34+31{e}^{-0.07\\cdot 7}≈{53}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}]}
